    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP on July 31st is headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (82.87), who presents our highest ceiling today. Facing Bibee\u2019s 4.46 xERA, **Corbin Carroll** leverages both the favorable matchup and his leadoff spot to anchor our outfield, maximizing his opportunities to steal the bases we so desperately need. He is joined in the top tier by **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Brice Turang** (75.06). **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% boost due to park factors and platoon advantage against Wacha, while **Brice Turang**\u2019s efficiency is pushed into the elite range by a 19% SP skill advantage against Ryan Johnson, further bolstered by the stolen base potential against a battery with an unfavorable 1.89s pop time.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision landed in the middle infield, where **Jose Caballero** (59.99) narrowly edged out **Travis Bazzana** (47.69) for the starting spot. While **Jose Caballero** faces a tough test in Imanaga, his 13% platoon boost and status as an everyday starter outweighed the high-ERA matchup afforded to **Travis Bazzana**. We are also keeping a close eye on **Tyler Soderstrom**, who carries a strong 62.80 projection pending his lineup confirmation; his 10% park factor bump makes him a vital cog today. Conversely, we must note the ongoing risks regarding our \"Lineup Pending\" status for several players including **Cole Young**, **Brice Turang**, **Zach McKinstry**, and **Josh Naylor**. Any shifts in the batting order, particularly for **Cole Young** or **Brice Turang**, could impact their run-scoring upside. We remain sidelined with **Trevor Story** and **Seiya Suzuki** due to injury and rest, respectively, while **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte** stay in reserve as we carefully manage our limited daily game caps.",
